  "evidence": "Traced the selected central-invariance proof from the signed differential form through fundamental-unit logarithms, interval integration, basepoint independence and the preserved moving-frame estimate. The cyclic subgroup calculation in an explicit totally complex field checks the sign and normalization.",
  "claims": [
    {
      "id": "CAV-01",
      "title": "The residual torus and its probability measure",
      "chapter": "inputs",
      "anchor": "inputs-central-average",
      "statement": "In a totally complex field with r infinite places, the effective central scaling directions have logarithms in ℝ^r. Common scaling is already an invariance, and logarithms of the units form a lattice Λ in the sum-zero hyperplane V. Averaging over the fundamental-unit coordinates with Lebesgue measure on [0,1] in each gives Haar probability on V/Λ and produces a form invariant under every complex scalar tuple.",
      "hypotheses": "The starting form is continuous on the positive cone, invariant under the arithmetic GL group and under tuples of complex scalars of equal modulus. The general unit theorem uses place multiplicities; in this application they are all two.",
      "definitions": [
        "inverse-adjoint action H_w↦|z_w|^(−2)H_w",
        "the pullback includes the linear action on every tangent argument",
        "logarithmic embedding of the units, fundamental logs and the complement to the constant direction"
      ],
      "equations": [
        "inputs-central-unit-average"
      ],
